Kept this one direct. Try the longest possible proper prefix first, compare it with the suffix of the same length, and stop at the first match. This is easy to follow, although repeated substring comparisons can make it slow for a long repeated string.

The code does not modify `s`. It also handles strings shorter than two characters before accessing any substring.

```cpp
#include <string>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    string lps(string s) {
        int n = static_cast<int>(s.size());

        for (int length = n - 1; length >= 1; --length) {
            if (s.compare(0, length, s, n - length, length) == 0) {
                return s.substr(0, length);
            }
        }

        return "";
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` in the worst case because many candidate lengths may be compared.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the returned string, with `O(1)` extra working space.

Here is a less usual dynamic programming version. `match[i][j]` stores how many equal characters continue from positions `i` and `j`. For every possible prefix length, the table can then check the matching suffix in constant time.

Dry run for `s = "aaaa"`: the prefix lengths 1, 2, and 3 all match their suffixes, but length 3 is the largest proper one, so the result is `"aaa"`.

Empty and one-character strings return `""`. The input is not changed. This is mainly a clear alternative for learning, since its memory use is much higher than KMP or the Z-array.

```python
class Solution:
    def lps(self, s: str) -> str:
        n = len(s)
        if n < 2:
            return ""

        match = [[0] * (n + 1) for _ in range(n + 1)]

        for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):
            for j in range(n - 1, -1, -1):
                if s[i] == s[j]:
                    match[i][j] = 1 + match[i + 1][j + 1]

        for length in range(n - 1, 0, -1):
            suffix_start = n - length
            if match[0][suffix_start] >= length:
                return s[:length]

        return ""
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` to fill the table and check the candidates.
Space complexity: `O(n^2)` for the dynamic programming table.

The last value in the KMP prefix table already stores the length we need. For each position, the table keeps the longest prefix that is also a suffix of the part seen so far. On a mismatch, the previous table values avoid starting the comparison from zero again.

The input string is only read, not changed. A string with one character returns an empty result because the whole string cannot be used.

```java
class Solution {
    public String lps(String s) {
        int n = s.length();
        if (n < 2) {
            return "";
        }

        int[] prefixTable = new int[n];
        int matched = 0;

        for (int i = 1; i < n; ) {
            if (s.charAt(i) == s.charAt(matched)) {
                ++matched;
                prefixTable[i] = matched;
                ++i;
            } else if (matched > 0) {
                matched = prefixTable[matched - 1];
            } else {
                prefixTable[i] = 0;
                ++i;
            }
        }

        return s.substring(0, prefixTable[n - 1]);
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the prefix table.

The Z-array tells how many characters from the beginning match at every position. A valid answer is a match that reaches the end of the string. Checking positions from left to right means the first such match is the longest one.

Dry run with `s = "ababab"`:
- At index 2, the matching length is 4.
- The match reaches the last character, so the answer is `"abab"`.

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    string lps(string s) {
        int n = static_cast<int>(s.size());
        if (n < 2) {
            return "";
        }

        vector<int> z(n, 0);
        int left = 0;
        int right = 0;

        for (int i = 1; i < n; ++i) {
            if (i <= right) {
                z[i] = min(right - i + 1, z[i - left]);
            }

            while (i + z[i] < n && s[z[i]] == s[i + z[i]]) {
                ++z[i];
            }

            if (i + z[i] - 1 > right) {
                left = i;
                right = i + z[i] - 1;
            }

            if (i + z[i] == n) {
                return s.substr(0, z[i]);
            }
        }

        return "";
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the Z-array.
